Universality and Convergence of Generative Flows Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.05490v1 [cs.LG] 04 Oct 2026 Universality and Convergence of Generative Flows Leo Brunswic Affiliation: LLM Suite Team, JP Morgan Chase & Co. leo.brunswic@jpmorgan.com Abstract Generative flows sample from an unnormalized target by training a flow to be balanced, and the training loss is the signal a practitioner watches. We ask what that signal is worth: whether a small loss certifies an accurate sampler, whether the loss can be driven to zero, and how fast gradient descent does so. The loss decides the first. Losses that compare the two sides of the balance by their difference bound, in total variation, the error of the sampler the flow implies, with explicit constants that do not involve the policy; flow-matching losses that compare them through a ratio admit no such bound, already on a single cycle, whenever their generator is continuous at balance. On graphs, the backward policy decides the other two. Once it is frozen, balance becomes invariance under the backward chain, so that existence is free on finite graphs, and one constant — the norm of that chain’s Green operator, which plays the role of an inverse spectral gap — fixes the order of the curvature of the loss around the balanced flow, from above and below, and sets a floor under the rate at which training converges near it. The mechanism is that gradient descent diffuses the flow along the backward policy. For the squared-logarithm generator of detailed and trajectory balance, training the balance loss on states converges globally on every finite path-connected graph, from every positive initialization. The constant can be infinite while backward trajectories are short on average, and exact flow matching can then fail. The bounds and rates are tested by exact computation on enumerable state spaces, and every theorem carries a certification status computed from a Lean 4 development. Disclaimer: This paper was prepared for informational purposes by the LLM Suite group of JP Morgan Chase and its affiliates (‘JPMC’) and is not a product of the Research Department of JP Morgan. JP Morgan makes no representation, warranty or undertaking whatsoever and disclaims all liability for the completeness, accuracy or reliability of the information contained herein. This document is not intended as investment research or investment advice, or a recommendation, offer or solicitation for the purchase or sale of any security, financial instrument, financial product or service, or to be used in any way for evaluating the merits of participating in any transaction, and shall not constitute a solicitation under any jurisdiction or to any person, if such solicitation under such jurisdiction or to such person would be unlawful. 1 Introduction 1.1 Generative flows and the cost of mixing A generative flow or GFlowNet ( Bengio et al., 2021 ; Bengio et al., 2023 ) was originally defined on a directed acyclic graph as a positive weighting of its edges, called a flow. It defines a sampling process for an unnormalized target distribution on the vertices: it starts from some initial distribution on the vertices and, at each vertex, chooses between following an edge and stopping (which we see as following an edge toward a sink state, weighted by the reward), with probabilities proportional to the flow. The fundamental result of GFlowNets states that when the flow is balanced — at every state, what arrives or starts equals what leaves or stops — the stopped state is an exact sample of the target ( Bengio et al., 2021 ; Bengio et al., 2023 ) . Training enforces balance through a loss: flow matching, detailed balance, trajectory balance and their variants ( Bengio et al., 2021 ; Bengio et al., 2023 ; Whitammer et al., 2022 ; Pan et al., 2023 ; Madan et al., 2023 ; da Silva et al., 2024 ; Hu et al., 2025 ) . Built on directed acyclic graphs, the framework now covers continuous state spaces ( Li et al., 2023 ; Lahlou et al., 2023 ) , state spaces with cycles ( Brunswic et al., 2024 ; Brunswic et al., 2025a ; Morozov et al., 2025 ) and multiple agents ( Brunswic et al., 2025b ) , and the models it fine-tunes are moving the same way. Generative flows fine-tune autoregressive models, whose generation explores a tree ( Yu et al., 2025 ; Takase et al., 2024 ; Ho et al., 2024 ; Bartoldson et al., 2025 ; Wang et al., 2026 ) ; diffusion language models unmask tokens in any order and some revise them ( Sahoo et al., 2024 ; Nie et al., 2025 ; von Rütte et al., 2025 ) , so a state is reached along many paths and, where tokens are revised, along cycles. In their seminal work, Bengio et al. (2021) explain that GFlowNets are expected to escape the cost of mixing that burdens Markov chain Monte Carlo ( Levin & Peres, 2017 ) during inference . A common informal belief is that the choice of the frozen backward policy used in the detailed-balance and trajectory-balance losses matters for the convergence of training ( Gritsaev et al., 2025 ) . Whoever trains a generative flow watches a loss and needs three things from it: that a small value certifies an accurate sampler, that the value can be driven to zero, and that gradient descent drives it there in reasonable time. Answers so far are partial: da Silva et al. (2025b) ; da Silva et al. (2025a) give guarantees on finite acyclic graphs, Brunswic et al. (2025a) bound the total-variation sampling error of any flow by its flow-matching defect and prove L 1 L^{1} -universality of ergodic flows, and Morozov et al. (2025) show that on a finite graph a positive frozen backward policy determines a unique balanced flow, so that the stability of the loss plays no role in where training goes. These last two works point the same way: holding a policy fixed is what makes universality and existence provable. The present work combines these ideas with the Markov-chain viewpoint of Deleu & Bengio (2023) into an encompassing framework that formalizes the intuition that frozen backward policies matter for training convergence; we provide convergence bounds controlling the sampling error by losses and describe the training dynamics in terms of a frozen backward policy. What a small loss certifies does not depend on the policy, and on finite graphs neither does existence; every rate of training does, through the mixing of the frozen backward chain and the ratio of the training measure to its invariant measure, and near balance that mixing constant bounds the curvature of the loss from both sides: GFlowNets do not escape the cost of mixing during training . Contributions. 1. Certificates and their limit (Section 2 ). We bound the sampling error by the stable and KL-weak-FM losses, with constants that do not involve the policy. We show that ratio-based flow matching with a generator continuous at 1 1 admits no such bound, already on one cycle. 2. The frozen chain (Section 3 ). We freeze the backward policy and wrap the graph around, so that balance is the invariance of one Markov chain. The norm B ^ \widehat{B} of that chain’s Green operator is the constant of the paper; it is a classical object ( Kemeny & Snell, 1960 ; Meyer, 1975 ; Meyer, 1980 ) , and what is new is its role in the curvature and the rate of training. 3. Existence (Section 4 ). On a finite path-connected graph a positive frozen backward policy admits, for each target positive on the stopping states, a balanced flow sampling it exactly, with no mixing hypothesis ( Morozov et al., 2025 ) ; we reprove it through the invariant measure of the loop closure ( Deleu & Bengio, 2023 ) and certify it. On a general state space, a frozen backward policy that mixes summably makes its flows universal in L p L^{p} . 4. Dynamics (Section 5 and Appendix B ). We show that gradient descent diffuses the flow along the frozen backward policy, that the curvature of the loss around the balanced flow is of order 1 / B ^ 2 1/\widehat{B}^{2} from both sides, and derive the local rate; for the generator ( log ⁡ x ) 2 (\log x)^{2} , training the balance loss on states converges from every positive initialization on every finite path-connected graph. We show that freezing does not give stability. 5. The boundary (Section 6 ). We give a counterexample, the doubling graph, on which the choice of the frozen backward policy alone decides whether a balanced flow exists and whether the constants of the bounds are finite. 6. A certified paper (Section 1.3 ). Every theorem of the paper is certified in Lean 4. Every theorem statement and badge is generated from the Lean certificate, and the whole development is unrolled in an appendix that opens on its proof-structure graph. With a fixed backward policy on a directed acyclic graph, learning a generative flow is entropy-regularized reinforcement learning ( Tiapkin et al., 2024 ; Deleu et al., 2024 ; Mohammadpour et al., 2024 ) , and entropy-regularized policy gradient converges globally at linear rates in tabular discounted Markov decision processes ( Mei et al., 2020 ; Cen et al., 2022 ) , and Morozov et al. (2025) extend the equivalence to graphs with cycles; the analysis here works in the space of flows, allows cycles and names the constant that sets the rate. Gritsaev et al. (2025) train the backward policy this paper freezes. 1.2 Generative flows: framework and notation We work in the measurable theory of generative flows of Brunswic et al. (2024) , which admits cycles, in the reward-free form of Brunswic et al. (2025a) and Brunswic et al. (2025b) . One set of definitions then covers graphs, where the background measure is the counting measure, and continuous state spaces, where it is the Lebesgue or Riemannian volume; it is also the setting of the Lean development, whose sampler results hold on an arbitrary measura