ward policy answers the other two, once the graph is made a chain. The framework of Section 1.2 admits cycles, and the results on the frozen chain are proved for a Markov chain with an invariant measure, the ergodic presentation of generative flows ( Brunswic et al., 2025a ) that this paper adopts, and stated here on the graph. The acyclic graphs of practice reduce to it by a variant of the loop trick of Deleu & Bengio (2023) : wrap the graph around by an edge from the sink back to the source. On a finite path-connected graph the sampler’s trajectories, read backward, become the excursions of one irreducible chain, the balanced flow becomes its invariant measure, and cutting the wrap returns the acyclic flow, so the reduction loses nothing; what it adds, a chain that is never reversible and may be periodic, is why the constant is the norm B ^ \widehat{B} of the chain’s Green operator and not a spectral gap (Section 3 ). Balance is then the invariance of that chain, existence is free on finite graphs, and B ^ \widehat{B} is the cost of mixing that training pays: gradient descent, a diffusion along the backward policy, sees a curvature it bounds from both sides, a local rate it guarantees and, through hitting times, a guaranteed global one. The doubling graph closes the story where B ^ \widehat{B} is infinite. Cited inputs (Section 1.2 ). Sampling theorem : a balanced flow samples its terminal flow exactly. Forcing balance : a flow that is not balanced, stopped by its implied terminal flow, samples with an error set by its initial defect; both certificates start there. What a small loss certifies (Section 2 ). Stable losses certify the forced sampler, with a constant free of the policy. KL-weak-FM certifies it from samples of the target, to leading order by the square root of the loss’s excess over the target’s self-entropy. Ratio losses cannot : flow matching with a generator continuous at 1 1 is blind to circulations, and already on one cycle, for every target but one, no function of the loss that vanishes with it bounds the sampling error. The frozen chain and existence. The Green operator (Section 3 ): its norm B ^ \widehat{B} is the constant of the paper, bounded through the hitting times of backward trajectories or through mixing. Existence on finite graphs (Section 4 ): on a finite path-connected graph, for each target positive on the stopping states, a backward policy positive on the edges has a balanced flow that samples it exactly ( Morozov et al., 2025 ) , here the invariant measure of the loop closure. L p L^{p} -universality (Section 4 ): on a general state space a frozen backward policy with summable mixing has flows universal in L p L^{p} , exactly so in L ∞ L^{\infty} — the one place where the mixing sum, and not B ^ \widehat{B} , is the price. Gradient descent diffuses the flow (Section 5 ). Gradient as diffusion : near balance the gradient of the flow-matching loss on a graph applies the backward chain’s I − P I-P twice, once through its adjoint. Curvature and local rate : near balance the curvature of the flow-matching loss is of order 1 / B ^ 2 1/\widehat{B}^{2} from both sides, and training on the flow-matching and detailed-balance losses converges at a rate at least of that order, linearized on any state space and in full on a finite graph. Global convergence : the ( log ⁡ x ) 2 (\log x)^{2} balance loss on states converges from every positive initialization on a finite path-connected graph, at a rate read off backward trajectories and with no mixing hypothesis. Non-monotonicity : freezing buys curvature, not stability; a circulation can still lower these losses, and for strictly unimodal generators gaining circulation is how training reaches balance. The boundary (Section 6 ). The doubling graph : its backward trajectories have finite mean length, the quantity Morozov et al. (2025) minimize, with a trained backward policy, to keep sampling short, yet B ^ \widehat{B} is infinite in every L p L^{p} and exact